      \field{abstract}{The log of the price of a share is commonly modelled as a Brownian motion with drift, {$\sigma$} Bt + ct, where the constants c and {$\sigma$} are unknown. In order to use the Black-Scholes option pricing formula, one needs an estimate of {$\sigma$}, though not of c. In this paper, we propose a new estimator of {$\sigma$} based on the high, low, and closing prices in a day's trading. This estimator has the merit of being unbiased whatever the drift c. In common with other estimators of {$\sigma$}, the approximation of the true high and low values of the drifting Brownian motion by the high and low values of a random walk introduces error, often quite a serious error. We shall show how a simple correction can overcome this error almost completely.}
